{% extends 'base.en.html.twig' %}
{% set nav_actif = 'puer' %}
{% set route_fr = 'paleo_puer' %}
{% set route_de = 'paleo_de_puer' %}

{% block titre %}Puer natus est{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Puer natus est, introit of the Mass of Christmas Day: a first chant for reading neumes step by step in the Graduale Triplex.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>Example 1 — <span class="mot-latin">Puer natus est</span></h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    The entrance chant (introit) of the Mass of Christmas Day. A luminous, welcoming chant,
    perfect for reading your very first neumes.
</p>

<div class="fiche">
    <dl>
        <dt>Genre</dt><dd>Introit (entrance chant / <span class="mot-latin">antiphona ad introitum</span>)</dd>
        <dt>Occasion</dt><dd>Mass of Christmas Day (<span class="mot-latin">in die</span>), 25 December</dd>
        <dt>Text</dt><dd>After the prophet Isaiah (Is 9, 6) and psalm 97</dd>
        <dt>Mode</dt><dd>Mode VII (<em>sol</em> mode, called «&nbsp;angelic&nbsp;», bright and soaring)</dd>
        <dt>Style</dt><dd>Rather <strong>neumatic</strong>&nbsp;: a few notes per syllable</dd>
        <dt>In the <em>Triplex</em></dt><dd>p. 47</dd>
    </dl>
</div>

<div class="lecteur-audio">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen to the chant</span>
    <p class="note-audio">Three interpretations, from the monastic style to the historically informed approach&nbsp;—&nbsp;ideal for comparison.</p>
    <div class="videos-audio">
        <figure class="video-yt">
            <div class="ratio">
                <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/Kz1IUxAxYRw" title="Puer natus est — Monks of Solesmes" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </div>
            <figcaption><strong>Choir of the Monks of Saint-Pierre de Solesmes Abbey</strong></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="video-yt">
            <div class="ratio">
                <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/h_Ez91zGm4Y" title="Puer natus est — Ensemble Organum, Marcel Pérès" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </div>
            <figcaption><strong>Ensemble Organum</strong>, dir. Marcel Pérès — <em>Chant de l'Église de Rome (6th–13th c.)</em></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="video-yt">
            <div class="ratio">
                <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/yt9PKLNllMA" title="How to sing the introit Puer natus est?" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </div>
            <figcaption>Tutorial&nbsp;: «&nbsp;How to sing the introit <em>Puer natus est</em>&nbsp;?&nbsp;»</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Puer natus est nobis,<br>et filius datus est nobis&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">A child is born to us,<br>and a son is given to us&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">cujus imperium super humerum ejus&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">sovereignty rests upon his shoulder&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">et vocabitur nomen ejus magni consilii Angelus.</div>
    <div class="fr">and he shall be called the Messenger of the great design.</div>
</div>

<h2>What to look at first</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Let us start gently, with the first word&nbsp;: <span class="mot-latin">Pu-er</span>. It
    has two syllables, so at least two «&nbsp;packets&nbsp;» of neumes. The beauty of
    Gregorian chant is that a single syllable can carry several notes. Our job&nbsp;: for
    each syllable, to spot <em>how many</em> notes there are and <em>which way</em> they
    move.
</p>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">Method in 3 steps</span>
    <p>
        1) Find the syllable in the text. 2) In the middle (square notation), count the
        squares and follow them up and down. 3) Glance above (Laon) and below (St.
        Gall)&nbsp;: should you press on, settle, lighten&nbsp;? That is all.
    </p>
</div>

<h3>Syllable by syllable (start of the chant)</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong><span class="mot-latin">Pu-</span></strong>&nbsp;: the chant typically takes
        flight with a rising movement. Look here for a <strong>pes</strong> (two notes going
        up) — the voice «&nbsp;takes off&nbsp;».
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><span class="mot-latin">-er</span></strong>&nbsp;: the voice settles, often
        with a <strong>clivis</strong> (two notes going down) or a small group coming back
        down to the supporting note.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><span class="mot-latin">na-tus</span></strong>&nbsp;: listen to how the word
        «&nbsp;born&nbsp;» is brought out&nbsp;; the neumes become more generous here
        (several notes), underlining the most important word of the sentence.
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Reminder — accent and music</span>
    <p>
        In chant the music follows the meaning of the text&nbsp;: important words (<span
        class="mot-latin">Puer</span>, «&nbsp;the child&nbsp;») often receive more notes or
        a slight leaning. This is not decorative&nbsp;: it is a way of <em>proclaiming</em>
        the text.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Seeing the three notations</h2>
<figure class="facsimile">
    <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/puer-natus-est.jpg') }}" alt="Puer natus est in square notation, Codex Gisle">
    <figcaption>The introit "Puer natus est" in square notation, <em>Codex Gisle</em> (illuminated gradual of Gisela von Kerssenbrock, circa 1300, Rulle). Source: Wikimedia Commons, public domain.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="legende-triplex">
    <span><span class="pastille laon"></span>Laon, above (the rhythm)</span>
    <span><span class="pastille vat"></span>Square notation, centre (the notes)</span>
    <span><span class="pastille sg"></span>St. Gall, below (the gesture)</span>
</div>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point for this chant</span>
    <p>
        <span class="mot-latin">Puer natus est</span> is a <strong>neumatic</strong>
        chant&nbsp;: small groups of 2 to 4 notes per syllable. It is the ideal training
        ground for recognising the <strong>pes</strong>, the <strong>clivis</strong> and the
        <strong>torculus</strong> without being overwhelmed. The next chant will multiply
        the notes…
    </p>
</div>

